---
title: Configure & Regenerate Feature Flags
---

Now that you know how `config/app-config.json` is structured, it's time to edit it in your local clone and regenerate the Kotlin configuration that the app actually reads.

## Edit a Flag

In your local clone of `vonage-video-android-app`, open `config/app-config.json` and change one or more flags in `meetingRoomSettings`. For example, turn off screen sharing and switch the default layout to a grid:

```json
{
  "meetingRoomSettings": {
    "allowScreenShare": false,
    "defaultLayoutMode": "grid"
  }
}
```

Save the file once you've made your edit.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Leave `"baseApiUrl": "${BASE_API_URL}"` untouched — it's substituted automatically from `local.properties` or the `BASE_API_URL` environment variable you set up in the previous step.
{% /aside %}

## Regenerate the Build Configuration

From the root of your local clone, run the config generator Gradle task:

```sh
./gradlew generateVonageConfig
```

This reads `config/app-config.json` and regenerates:

- `app/build/generated/source/jsonConfig/com/vonage/android/config/AppConfig.kt` — runtime Kotlin constants
- `gradle/generated-config.properties` — build properties used for product flavor selection

{% aside type="caution" %}
Never hand-edit `gradle/generated-config.properties` — it is always overwritten by `generateVonageConfig`. If you need to force a regeneration after a change that doesn't seem to have picked up, run:

```sh
./gradlew clean generateVonageConfig
```
{% /aside %}

## Rebuild the App

Because some flags select entirely different Gradle product flavor variants (for example, disabling `allowChat` swaps in the `vonage-feature-chat` module's `disabled` variant), do a full rebuild rather than an incremental one:

```sh
./gradlew clean installDebug
```

Or in Android Studio: **Build → Clean Project**, then run the app again with the ▶️ Run button.

Next, you'll confirm your changes in the running app.
